An example is a preview until it has been applied on its platform; every one passes `tofu
validate` and `tofu test` (its `tests/`, each run at `command = plan` under mocked providers; `make
examples-test` runs them all). Each pins its providers exactly, with their checksums in its
`.terraform.lock.hcl`.

Every root, in order:

1. `stowage_vm_seed` makes the VM's first-boot material once (its `lifecycle` ignores later
   changes: the VM upgrades by `stowage_vm_install`'s `version`), with `disk_dir` on a hypervisor,
   whose disk is a file (`disk_file`);
2. on a hypervisor, `seed-iso.sh` (an `external` data source: `xorriso` and `jq`) writes the
   NoCloud seed ISO of `user_data` and `meta_data`, the same bytes for the same seed; on a cloud,
   `user_data` is the instance's user data;
3. the platform's resources make the VM: its disk from `disk[<platform>]` (a hypervisor's import
   of `disk_file`; a cloud's image, the one `ship --disks` registered — `cloud_image`, looked up by
   name — else `image_id`, which a cloud's `image/` root registers), sized `os_gib` plus `app_gib`;
   its NICs by `nic_roles` (the roles, the primary first: known at plan, as the seed is made in
   the same apply; a release naming more than one NIC with a role missing there is refused), each
   with the MAC a hypervisor's root's `nic_macs` gives its role (role → MAC, given to the seed too:
   its `interfaces`, `mac=`, after the role's own spec in `seed_interfaces`, which then gives no
   `mac=` of its own, refused otherwise), else the `mac` the seed's `nics` gives it (`nic_roles`
   left out: one NIC, the primary's, by `nic_macs` or the seed's MAC, else the platform's own —
   TrueNAS's root derives one from the name — for a release that declares no NIC or whose one role
   the seed gives none; that MAC goes to the primary alone, listed first: a role in `nic_roles`
   with no MAC, a secondary role the seed's `nics` gives no MAC — its fix `nic_macs`, then
   `-replace=stowage_vm_seed.vm`, as the seed is made once; or, an installer made with Stowage
   before 0.40.0, which gives a secondary NIC none, upgrading Stowage and shipping the release
   again —, a `nic_macs` MAC other than the one the seed's `nics` names its role by, and two NICs
   sharing a MAC — the seed's and those added after it — refuse the plan), on its `attach`
   network; its CPUs
   and memory from `size` (left out, the release's recommended; under `min` a precondition refuses
   the plan, under `recommended` a `check` warns); on a cloud, its firewall rules from `firewall`
   (SSH from `ssh_sources`, each of `open_ports` from its sources);
4. `stowage_vm_install`, depending on the VM (on TrueNAS, on its power resource), waits for the
   first boot under the seed's one-time key, gives the VM over SSH what goes there (secret inputs,
   `given_wo`, a registry login, a kit), waits for its first install, reads its own host key and
   retires the one-time key. A later `version` upgrades it, `interfaces` names a NIC added after
   the seed.

A destroy: `deletion_protection = false` applied first, then `tofu destroy`, which forgets the
install (its plan warns, before approval, when the registry holds no disk set to make the VM
again) and then removes the VM.

## TrueNAS

`truenas/truenas` declares the VM, its devices, its power, its zvol and a staging dataset. What it
cannot declare yet — a file put on the host, a disk image written into a zvol, a VM stopped before
its devices are deleted — goes through Stowage's documented stopgap steps, run by `local-exec`
with the release's installer for this machine, `stowage_vm_seed`'s `ctl_path`: `vm truenas
import-image` (the disk into a fresh zvol, by its digest; a zvol holding it already is left as it
is), `vm truenas seed` (the seed ISO, named by its digest) and, at destroy, `vm truenas stop` (the
VM stopped before its devices go: the path recorded at create runs, so the step first checks the
installer is on this machine — the provider's cache keeps the last eight installers used, and a
destroy's refresh of `stowage_vm_seed` fetches it again — and otherwise fails naming `tofu apply
-refresh-only`, from another machine `tofu apply`). They read `TRUENAS_ENDPOINT` and `TRUENAS_API_KEY` as the
provider does: no credential enters the state. A new disk image (a new seed) replaces the zvol,
its device and the stop step, the VM stopped first. `truenas_vm_power`'s `restart_triggers`
restart the VM when what it reads at boot changes: a NIC added (`extra_nics`, which
`stowage_vm_install`'s `interfaces` then names), the disk, the seed, the size. The steps go once
`truenas/truenas` covers the three.

